---
/* The Map. One map, three questions.

   This archive had grown three of them off the same ground — the research map
   (1,235 record-book places, «how far have we got with the shelf»), the atlas
   (160 places, «where can we put a named person») and the graves map (64,
   «where is this family in the ground»). Eighty-four places were on more than
   one of them and twelve were on all three, and once the people layer was
   added to the research map in September 2026 one map was a strict superset
   of another. Three pages, three geocoders, three legends.

   They are one map now. Every marker carries up to three categories and the
   switch above the map says which one is being coloured. The PANEL never
   switches: a place that is a parish, a home and a grave says all three things
   at once, which is the whole reason for merging them.

   The map, the panel and the filters are still the kit's Atlas, untouched;
   the switching is done from outside it, through each marker's own category,
   in AtlasTimelineTint. */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Atlas from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Atlas.astro";
import AtlasTimelineTint from "../../components/AtlasTimelineTint.astro";
import { u } from "../../lib/url.js";
import { md, mdp } from "../../lib/md.js";
import r from "../../data/researchmap.json";
import mp from "../../data/map.json";
import a from "../../data/atlas.json";
import g from "../../data/gravesmap.json";
import mat from "../../data/matricula.json";
import ros from "../../data/roster.json";

const s = r.stats;
const st = r.stats;
/* Three cuts of this map, and the third is the one that answers the question.

   First: three coverage states, which saturated the moment the cataloguing
   finished — 1,219 of 1,239 places went gold. Second: shade by DEPTH, one or
   two volumes against ten or more, which looked better and said less. A
   parish with forty volumes nobody has opened and a parish with two nobody
   has opened are in exactly the same state, and «one or two volumes» told a
   reader nothing about whether the work had been done.

   Third, and current: the colour says HOW FAR WE HAVE GOT. Depth is not lost
   — it is in the panel, in words, where "114 volumes catalogued" can sit next
   to "nobody has opened any of it" without either pretending to be the other. */
const shelfCats = [
  ["none",       "In no catalogue at all",      "#8A8F96"],
  ["untouched",  "Catalogued, never opened",    "#C0392B"],
  ["worked",     "Researched, books unopened",  "#E8792B"],
  ["started",    "A volume of its own read",    "#EFC03B"],
  ["done",       "Every volume read",           "#1E8449"],
];
/* The atlas's own six, which are not coverage states at all — they say HOW we
   know a place, not how far through it we are. */
const peopleCats = [
  ["spine",     "The documented line",          "#4A1D7A"],
  ["carnia",    "Carnia — where it may start",  "#8E44AD"],
  ["sweep",     "Istria, where the name is",    "#B07CD9"],
  ["other",     "Other families of the name",   "#C9B6DC"],
  ["senj",      "The Senj and Karlobag side",   "#6C5CE0"],
  ["diaspora",  "Where they went",              "#D64FA0"],
];
const gravesCats = [
  ["stone",     "A headstone, photographed",    "#2E86C1"],
  ["register",  "A line in a burial register",  "#0B3D5C"],
  ["both",      "Both, at one place",           "#17A2B8"],
];
/* Every colour the map can use, in one list, because the kit colours a marker
   by looking its category up in the set it was given. «_none» is the category
   a marker takes when the question being asked is not one it can answer; its
   pill is hidden and switched off once, and the kit's own filter does the
   rest. */
/* The records layer. Findmypast is not a register anyone can open and it is
   not where the family lived — it is a record of a person at a place on a
   date. One colour, because the question it answers has one answer. */
const recordCats = [
  ["record",    "A record names somebody here", "#C77F0A"],
];
const cats = [...shelfCats, ...peopleCats, ...gravesCats, ...recordCats, ["_none", "", "#000"]];
const catOf = Object.fromEntries([
  ...shelfCats.map(c => [c[0], "shelf"]),
  ...peopleCats.map(c => [c[0], "people"]),
  ...gravesCats.map(c => [c[0], "graves"]),
  ...recordCats.map(c => [c[0], "records"]),
]);
const m0 = mp.stats;
/* Layers, not views. A place that answers two questions is still one dot —
   drawing it twice is exactly what merging the three maps was for — so it
   takes the colour of the rarest question it answers: the ground before our
   people, our people before the shelf. */
const schemes = [
  ["shelf",  "The shelf",  "1,235 parishes · how far we have got with the registers"],
  ["people", "Our people", "167 towns · where a named person can be put"],
  ["graves", "The ground", "61 places · where the family is actually buried"],
  ["records", "The records", `${m0.records} places · ${m0.recordsN} Findmypast records of somebody of the name`],
];
const m = mp.stats;
const abroad = m.peopleAbroad;
/* The living, counted off the roster rather than typed into a sentence that
   will be wrong the next time David tells us where somebody is. */
const living = ros.rows.filter(r => r.living && (r.places || []).length);
const liv = living.length;
const at = (t) => living.filter(r => (r.places || []).some(p => p.startsWith(t))).length;
const jhb = at("Johannesburg"), bne = at("Brisbane");
const LAB = { rc: "Roman Catholic", orth: "Orthodox", gc: "Greek Catholic",
              jew: "Jewish", mil: "Military", ref: "Reformed", civil: "Civil",
              ev: "Evangelical" };
/* md.js deliberately has no link syntax — the archive's data files are written
   in a markup that renders bold and italic and nothing else. This paragraph had
   two links written in it anyway, and both were printing as literal brackets on
   the live page. Rather than teach the shared renderer a new syntax that every
   other page would inherit, the links are resolved here, where they are used,
   and put through the same base-path helper as every other internal link. */
const link = (h) => h.replace(/\[([^\]]+)\]\((\/[^)]*)\)/g,
  (_, t, href) => `<a href="${u(href)}">${t}</a>`);
const layers = Object.entries(s.byLayer).sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]);
const pct = (n) => ((100 * n) / s.places).toFixed(1);
/* The cross-provider check. A gap can only be SEEN where two providers describe
   the same place, which today is six places in Istria — Pazin's own register
   list against FamilySearch's films. Everything after 1890 is the privacy
   cut-off and is not a gap at all; everything before about 1720 is a real book
   nobody has photographed. The page separates them rather than reporting 460. */
const KND = { b: "Births", m: "Marriages", d: "Deaths" };
const gapPlaces = r.places
  .filter(p => (p.gapSpans || []).length)
  .map(p => ({
    name: p.name,
    early: p.gapSpans.filter(g => g.from < 1890),
    late:  p.gapSpans.filter(g => g.from >= 1890),
  }))
  .sort((a, b) => b.early.length - a.early.length || a.name.localeCompare(b.name));
const nEarly = gapPlaces.reduce((a, p) => a + p.early.reduce((x, g) => x + g.n, 0), 0);
const nLate  = gapPlaces.reduce((a, p) => a + p.late.reduce((x, g) => x + g.n, 0), 0);
const yrs = (g) => (g.from === g.to ? `${g.from}` : `${g.from}–${g.to}`);
---
<Base
  title="The Map"
  eyebrow={`${m.places} places · ${s.volumes} register volumes catalogued · ${m.peoplePlaced} people placed · ${m.burials} burials`}
  dek="One map and four layers. Where are the record books and how far have we got with them; where can this archive put a named person; where is the family actually in the ground; and where does a record merely name somebody of the name on one day. Switch any of them on or off. A place that answers more than one question is still one dot, and it wears the colour of the rarest answer it has — so with everything on, the records, the graves and the people stand out of the shelf rather than hiding under it.">

  <div class="at-scheme">
    <div class="lab">Three layers — switch any of them on or off</div>
    <div class="at-sc-row"></div>
    <p class="at-sc-key">
      <span><i style="background:#C0392B"></i>Red through amber to green is <b>the shelf</b>, and red means nobody has opened it</span>
      <span><i style="background:#8E44AD"></i>Violet and pink is <b>our people</b></span>
      <span><i style="background:#2E86C1"></i>Blue is <b>the ground</b></span>
    </p>
  </div>

  <Atlas
    recordAtlas="https://daviddef.github.io/TheMap/place"
    years
    dataUrl={u("/map-data.json")}
    cats={cats}
    bounds={[[42.2, 13.2], [46.8, 19.6]]}
    homeLabel="Back to Croatia"
    placeholder="Brinje · Torrington · Otočac · Broken Hill"
    hint={`${m.places} places, merged out of ${m.sums.research + m.sums.atlas + m.sums.graves + m.sums.records} markers across four sources. ${m.merged} answer more than one of the three questions and ${m.allThree} answer all three — and with every layer on, those are the ones wearing the rarer colour.`}
  />

  <AtlasTimelineTint dataUrl={u("/map-data.json")} schemes={schemes} catOf={catOf} />

  <div class="one" style="border-left-color:var(--accent);margin-top:26px">
    <div class="lab">Why there is one map here and not three</div>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={link(mdp(
`This archive had grown **three maps off the same ground**. A map of the record books. A map of the people. A map of the graves. Each was built by its own script, each geocoded its own place names, and each had its own legend — so the same town was a dot on three pages and the reader had to hold all three in their head to see what was actually there.

**${m.sums.research} + ${m.sums.atlas} + ${m.sums.graves} markers are ${m.places} places.** ${m.merged} of them answer more than one question and **${m.allThree} answer all three**: Rijeka, Ližnjan, Pazin, Poreč, Omiš, Split, Pula, Rovinj, Bakar, Kaštelir, Sovinjak and Tar.

**Rijeka is the case for merging.** On one page it now says: sixty-nine register volumes catalogued and *not one of them opened*; ninety-six people the archive can put here; and **twenty-three burials**. Those were three separate facts on three separate pages, and the third of them — that we have twenty-three burials in a town whose registers we have never read — is only visible when they sit together.

**The layers change the colour. They never change the panel.** Click any marker, with any combination switched on, and it tells you everything it has: the registers, the people, the graves and the records together.

**The fourth layer is a different kind of claim, and it is drawn that way.** The shelf, our people and the ground are all statements about *where this family is*. [Findmypast](/findmypast/) is not: a census line, a landing, a marriage licence is **a record of somebody of the name at a place on one day**. ${m.recordsN} such records stand on ${m.records} places, in amber, and the panel calls them what they are — *named in a record here*, not *who the archive puts here*. A 1913 arrival at Ellis Island must never be allowed to look like a home. A further ${m.recordsUnplaced} records name only a state or a country and get no dot at all, for the same reason.

**A place that answers two questions is still one dot.** Drawing it twice is exactly what merging the three maps was for. It takes the colour of the **rarest question it answers** among the layers that are on — the ground before our people, our people before the shelf. There are 1,235 shelf places, 167 with people and 61 with graves, so the rare thing is the thing worth seeing and the common thing is the ground it sits on. **Rijeka answers all three and wears the burial colour; Gologorica answers two and wears the line's; Senjska Draga answers one and is sand.**

**Every view of this map is a link.** The layers, the basemap, whatever is typed in the find box, which filters are switched off and where the map is looking all live in the address bar, so a view can be sent to somebody rather than only remembered for you. **[Every parish where a book has actually been opened](/map/?layers=shelf&off=untouched,none,worked)** is six places and one link; so is **[the ground alone, on satellite](/map/?layers=graves&base=satellite)**. The button says *Copy link to this view*.

*It was a switch for a day* — one question at a time, and asking a second put the first away. David: «can our map rather layer the shelf, our people and the ground?»

*The old addresses still work.* [/research-map/](/research-map/), [/atlas/](/atlas/) and [/graves-map/](/graves-map/) all land here.

**And there was a map before any of these.** The Google My Map this family drew over years, imported whole from its KML and kept exactly as it was drawn — pins the Atlas has no place for, the movements David traced by hand, and the links that have since died. It is not a fourth layer, because it is not this archive's reading of anything: it is [the map that came first](/mymap/), kept as a record of what was already known before any of this was built.`))}></div>
  </div>

  <div class="one" style="margin-top:26px">
    <div class="lab">One provider at a time</div>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={mdp(
`The map above is every provider at once. **These show one each**, with the colours meaning exactly what they mean here — a parish that is sand on the FamilySearch view and absent from Pazin's is a parish whose books are filmed but whose originals nobody has listed.`)}></div>
    <div class="atlas-key" style="margin-top:14px">
      {r.sources.map(s => (
        <a class="at-cat on" href={u(`/map/${s.key}/`)} style="text-decoration:none">
          {s.label} — {st.bySource[s.key].places} places, {st.bySource[s.key].volumes} volumes
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="one" style="border-left-color:#7A5CA8;margin-top:26px">
    <div class="lab">The second question: the towns of our people</div>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={link(mdp(
`Ask the map about **our people** and every marker re-colours. **${m.people} towns**, holding **${m.peoplePlaced} placements of named people**, and **${abroad} of the ${m.people} are outside Croatia altogether** — Mione and Gologorica, but also Torrington, Memphis, Pfullingen, Broken Hill, Adelaide, Horsham and Brisbane. Use the **Whole world** button at the top right of the map to see them.

**The six colours here are not coverage states.** They say *how the archive knows a place* — the documented line, Carnia, the Istrian sweep, the Senj and Karlobag side, the diaspora, and other families of the name. Weight on this map is not kinship: a parish can hold forty people of the surname and be connected to this family by nothing whatever.

**${m.bySchemes["people + shelf"] || 0} of these towns also carry a shelf**, and **${m.allThree} carry all three questions at once**. They are one marker, not two: click it and it tells you the registers, the people and the graves together.

**Nothing here is geocoded twice.** The three maps this page replaced each resolved their own place names; they now share one set of places, and the coordinates come from a single gazetteer committed to the repository rather than from three different files in three different states.

**Living people are on this map by name and by town, and by nothing else.**`))}></div>
  </div>

  <h2 style="font-size:30px;margin-top:50px">Where this map comes from, and what it is worth</h2>

  <div class="one" style="border-left-color:var(--accent);margin-top:22px">
    <div class="lab">It is David's map, rebuilt because the original cannot be kept</div>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={mdp(
`David built a **Google My Map** of the Croatian record books over several years — FamilySearch first, then the other confessions — and drew in a second layer of Croatian genealogists working their own parishes. **A My Map dies with the account**, and it cannot be crawled.

So it was exported and rebuilt here. **That export is where the trouble starts.** The KML holds **1,709 placemarks**, but only **247 of them carry coordinates** — and of its 1,196 record-book places, only **ninety** do. The rest are *address pins*, which Google geocodes when it draws the map and not before. **As a backup, the export is about eight per cent of a map.**

What every pin does carry is a **FamilySearch browse waypoint** — **1,194 of them** — and a waypoint is the key that opens a parish's shelf. **That turned out to be the last thing his map was needed for.** Each confession root in the collection lists its own parishes, so the list is now walked from **FamilySearch's own tree** instead: 1,333 shelves, against his 1,288, and *Ždala* through *Zvoneće* that his export never had because the root returns exactly one thousand and stops.

His map still supplies what the tree does not: the **confessions**, drawn as he separated them, and **ninety coordinates**. The rest of the coordinates came from the **GeoNames** country dumps for Croatia and its seven neighbours — **${s.placed} of ${s.places}** places placed, without a single geocoding call.

**${s.places - s.placed} places are still unplaced** — they are mostly compound parish names like *Split-Lučac* or *Sv. Jakov-Šiljevica* that no gazetteer carries as one string.`)}></div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top:22px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px">
    <div class="one">
      <div class="lab">What the colours mean — and what they do not</div>
      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={mdp(
`**The colour says how far we have got with the SHELF, and nothing else.** Sand is *catalogued and never opened*. Teal is *researched and its own books never opened* — real work, done from print and index rather than from the registers. Gold means **a volume of this parish's own registers has been read**. Green is every volume, and **there is not a single green marker on this map**.

**That is the honest picture and it is meant to be uncomfortable.** ${st.byProgress.untouched} places catalogued and untouched · ${st.byProgress.worked} researched without their registers being opened · **${st.byProgress.started}** with a volume of their own read · **${st.byProgress.done} finished** · ${st.volumesRead} volumes of ${s.volumes} read one by one.

*Teal is new, and it exists because gold was lying.* Twenty-two markers were gold and **twenty of them had never had a single register volume opened**. They were gold because the search register NAMES them — a local history of Žminj; a migration paper that lists Žminj among the towns the Carni settled at. Reading a book **about** a parish and reading the parish's **own registers** are two different acts, and a scale whose legend says «how far we have got with the shelf» must not paint the first in the colour of the second. [Logged as a correction](/corrections/).

*This is the third scale this map has worn.* It carried three coverage states until the cataloguing finished and turned 1,219 of them the same colour; then it shaded by **depth** — one or two volumes against ten or more — which looked better and said less. **A parish with forty volumes nobody has opened and a parish with two nobody has opened are in the same state.** Depth now lives in the panel, in words, next to the sentence that says whether anybody has read it.

**«Read right through» can only be claimed where this archive knows which volumes were read** — which today is Antenati alone, because its catalogue carries a per-volume flag. Everywhere else a reading is recorded against the *place*, not the *book*, so the best that can honestly be said is «started». [That is on the work list](/worklist/).

**And the map cannot see what is filed abroad.** A Croatian town's registers sit wherever the state that kept them filed them: **Čakovec is under «Csáktornya», in a Hungarian county, inside a collection named for Slovenia**; five Croatian Istrian parishes are inside an Italian one. [Croatia, filed under other countries](/elsewhere/) lists what the walk of all 3,488 FamilySearch collections turned up.

**Grey is «in no catalogue at all», and every grey marker on it is now a checked negative.** They are places this archive **walked the FamilySearch catalogue for and found nothing** — Grožnjan, Lindar, Motovun, Višnjan, Villa Santina and Pirovac. A checked negative is worth more than a blank.

*Two of them used to be grey for a worse reason.* **Brekinjska and Hrastovac were Evangelical**, and this archive had written that it *«never found the Evangelical root in FamilySearch's waypoint tree»* — the shelf was not walked, not proved empty. **The root was found on 15 September and it was never hidden**: the collection's own waypoint list names it — **Evangelical (Evangelička crkva)**, one of ten confession roots. It holds **six parishes and seven volumes**, Brekinjska and Hrastovac among them, and all seven are now openable from this map. The lesson is the honest one — *the archive had not looked in the place the collection tells you to look.*

*The first cut of this map used three colours — not walked, catalogued, read.* It worked for about a day, and then the cataloguing finished and **1,219 of 1,239 places went gold**. A map where everything is one colour is not a map, so the shading was re-cut to carry something that still varies.

**Researched, books unopened** means a row in the [search register](${"/searched/"}) names the place as a source or a destination — and no volume of its registers has been read. That flag is **derived by matching place names against the register's own source and destination fields** — never against its prose, which mentions places for all sorts of reasons. A handful of names that are also ordinary Croatian words or surnames are excluded by hand. **It is approximate, and it will be wrong in both directions until every row is tagged properly.**`)}></div>
    </div>

    <div class="one">
      <div class="lab">The number this page exists to show</div>
      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={link(mdp(
`**${s.read} of ${s.places}.** That is how many places on this map have had *anything at all* read out of them — ${pct(s.read)} per cent. The other ${s.byProgress.untouched} are catalogued down to the volume and untouched.

**Seven providers, and they are not the same kind of thing.** ${r.sources.map(x => `**${x.label}** — ${st.bySource[x.key].walked} of ${st.bySource[x.key].places} places walked, ${st.bySource[x.key].volumes} volumes`).join(" · ")}.

FamilySearch is broad and shallow: a title, a year span, and **nothing name-indexed**. [Antenati](/antenati/) is the opposite shape again — two comuni, fifty-nine volumes, and every one of them a book to be turned by hand, because the Italian portal publishes no index of names at all. Its arks sit on a different host from everything else here, so the map sends you to [the archive's own list](/antenati/) rather than dressing them up as FamilySearch links. Pazin is narrow and deep, and it is the only one that admits what it has *not* done — **${st.notDigitised} of its volumes are marked «not digitised»**. *That column means «not digitised by Pazin» and nothing more*: checked against the films, **${st.notDigitisedButFilmed} of the ${st.notDigitised} have been photographed by FamilySearch anyway**, and only **${st.notDigitisedAnywhere}** are books that two catalogues agree nobody has filmed. **Every one of the ${s.volumes} catalogued volumes that FamilySearch has filmed is now openable from this map** — the ark harvest finished on 15 September, 5,482 of 5,482, with nothing left outstanding.

*This paragraph said something else for about an hour.* It reported that thirty-four of the forty Kvarner parishes had never been walked — **and they had been**. The builder was reading the wrong field: Kvarner keeps its volumes under a field called **films**, like Dalmatia, and carries a stale **books** title-list on six of its forty parishes. Reading **books** invented a gap that did not exist and under-counted the archive by thirty-four parishes and 201 volumes. [Logged as a correction](/corrections/).`))}></div>
    </div>

    <div class="one" style="border-left-color:var(--accent)">
      <div class="lab">Where two providers describe the same place, and disagree</div>
      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={link(mdp(
`**${st.crossChecked} places on this map are described by two real shelves**, and they are the only places where a hole can be seen at all. It was six, and all six were in Istria — Pazin's own register list against FamilySearch's films. It is ${st.crossChecked} now, and the comparisons have multiplied with the providers: **Istria against Pazin, Međimurje against the Slovenian collection, Croatian Istria against the Italian one, Delnice against the main Croatian shelf.**

**${nEarly} volumes are listed by one holder, from before 1890, that the other has not filmed.** Those are the ones worth caring about — books on a shelf with no photograph of them anywhere. Pazin's own list marks **${st.notDigitised} volumes «not digitised»**, of which **${st.notDigitisedAnywhere} are filmed by nobody else either** — the short list actually worth posting, set out place by place and holder by holder on [the undigitised](/undigitised/).

**${nLate} more fall after 1890.** They are **not** a gap. FamilySearch withholds recent registers for privacy and the other holder lists them because it holds the paper. Counting those as missing film would have turned a real finding of ${nEarly} volumes into a fake one of ${nEarly + nLate}.

*The test is deliberately strict about what counts as a shelf.* **The library catalogue is not one.** Its records are bibliographic lines that can span three centuries — «Matična knjiga, 1568-1917» is a single row standing for a parish's whole holdings — and letting it into the comparison inflated this test to seventy-eight places and 1,572 volumes, almost all of it a catalogue entry being read as a claim about a particular year. It is excluded, and the number came back down.

*And strict about what counts as cover.* An index is not a register and an allegato is not a register; a marriage index for 1815–1830 does not make the marriage book for those years present. Only births, marriages and deaths are compared, and only against their own kind.`))}></div>
      <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
        <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:13.5px">
          <thead><tr>
            <th style="text-align:left;padding:6px 10px 6px 0">Place</th>
            <th style="text-align:left;padding:6px 10px">Filmed nowhere, before 1890</th>
            <th style="text-align:left;padding:6px 0 6px 10px">Held back for privacy, after 1890</th>
          </tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {gapPlaces.map(p => (
              <tr style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule)">
                <td style="padding:8px 10px 8px 0;white-space:nowrap"><strong>{p.name}</strong></td>
                <td style="padding:8px 10px">
                  {p.early.length
                    ? p.early.map(g => `${KND[g.kind]} ${yrs(g)} (${g.n})`).join(" · ")
                    : "—"}
                </td>
                <td style="padding:8px 0 8px 10px;color:var(--dim)">
                  {p.late.length
                    ? p.late.map(g => `${KND[g.kind]} ${yrs(g)} (${g.n})`).join(" · ")
                    : "—"}
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">Volume counts in brackets. A span is merged from consecutive volumes, so «Births 1629–1714 (5)» is five books covering that stretch with nothing of their kind on film across it.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="one" style="border-left-color:var(--accent)">
      <div class="lab">What the four marks beside a volume actually claim</div>
      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={link(mdp(
`Every volume in a panel carries one of four marks, and **all four are statements about this archive's own reading** — none of them is a statement about the book, or about what anybody else has done with it.

**● read** — somebody here opened it and went through it end to end. There are **two** such volumes outside Antenati: Gračišće's baptism book 1667–1745, read image by image off Pazin's microfilm, and Donji Proložac's deaths 1837–1859, swept page by page.

**◐ part read** — opened, and some of it read: a stretch of years, a run of images, an index followed. Svetvinčenat's 1663–1708 and one Vodnjan reel.

**○ never opened** — **this archive keeps a per-volume record for this parish, and this volume is not in it.** Nobody here has read it. It is a checked negative about *our work*, and about nothing else: the book is filmed, it is linkable from this panel, and for all this archive knows somebody read it yesterday in Pazin.

**· not written down** — the archive keeps **no** per-volume record for this parish at all, so it cannot say either way. Most of this map is in this state.

**The difference between the last two is the difference between a negative and a silence**, and it is the whole reason there are four marks rather than three.

*And the honest limit of «never opened».* It is only as good as [the ledger](/searched/) behind it. A volume somebody read before that ledger existed, and never wrote down, would show as never opened — so the state means **«not recorded as read»**, and the archive's answer to that is to record readings the day they happen rather than the week after.`))}></div>
    </div>

    <div class="one">
      <div class="lab">Matricula — the nine Littoral volumes, and where each one opens</div>
      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={link(mdp(
`**Matricula Online publishes the Austrian Protestant registers of the Küstenland**, free and without an account, and until 17 September no map this archive keeps had them. **Nine volumes, four congregations, 1848 to 1918.**

**Pola is this family's ground and Trieste and Gorizia are not**, so the provider is drawn as what it is — a shelf that mostly overlaps nothing else, in the way [Antenati](/antenati/) and the Delnice films already do. They do not open from a marker, because the panel builds FamilySearch addresses and these are not FamilySearch; they open from here.`))}></div>
      <ul class="matlist">
        {mat.places.map((pl) => (
          <li>
            <b>{pl.name}{pl.also && pl.also.length ? ` · ${pl.also[0]}` : ""}</b>
            <ol>{pl.books.map(([sig, t, lo, hi]) => (
              <li><a href={`${mat.base}${pl.matricula}/${sig}/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{t} ↗</a></li>))}
            </ol>
          </li>))}
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="one">
      <div class="lab">The forty-one places this map cannot draw</div>
      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={link(mdp(
`**1,285 parishes are catalogued and 1,244 of them have a dot.** The other **forty-one carry 130 volumes between them and cannot be put on the ground**, because no gazetteer this archive can reach knows where they are.

They are small Croatian parishes — *Trojstvo, Viduševac, Taborsko, Sobotica, Prvić Šepurina, Juraj u Trnju, Hrnetić, Vlaška Kapela* — and a handful of institutions that are not settlements at all: **Bolnica Dubrovnik**, **Nahodište Dubrovnik** and **Nahodište Split**, which are a hospital and two foundling houses and should in the end hang on their towns.

*What was tried.* GeoNames files a village too small for a settlement record under its **church** or its **railway stop** instead, and reading those classes as well as the populated places rescued four — Mađarevo is a railway stop, Mandalina a railway halt, Komarevo a locality. **A stream or a hill of the same name is not the village** and is deliberately not used: the Glogovnica in the dumps is a river.

The rest need a Croatian source and a person, not another pass of the same script. They are listed by [the place audit](/method/), which runs the two tests that found six parishes standing in the wrong country.`))}></div>
    </div>

    <div class="one" style="border-left-color:var(--accent)">
      <div class="lab">«GAP» and «NOT FILMED ANYWHERE» are opposites, and neither means «no link»</div>
      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={link(mdp(
`Three different silences turn up in one panel and they were easy to confuse, so here is each one.

**GAP — no marriages register listed anywhere, 6 years.** Nobody has a book of that kind for those years. Not Pazin, not FamilySearch, not Antenati, not anyone this archive walks. **The book is missing, or it never existed.** This is the row that should make you unhappy; it is tinted for exactly that reason. *(It said «no register filmed» until 16 September, which was the wrong word and made it look like the next one.)*

**NOT FILMED ANYWHERE.** The opposite. **The book exists** — a catalogue names it, a shelf holds it — and **nobody has photographed it.** Nothing is lost; it is simply not readable from a desk. These are set out one by one on [the undigitised](/undigitised/), and they are the ones worth writing a letter about.

**A volume with no link at all.** A third thing again, and the commonest. **Gračišće is the example**: thirty-two volumes, every one of them listed by the Pazin State Archive, which publishes a register list and no images — and FamilySearch's Croatian collection does not hold the parish. So there is no address to link to and there never was one to harvest. The panel now says so in those words. Where a place's volumes *do* come from a filmed collection and still show no link, that is the other case: an address this archive has not fetched yet, and the panel says that instead.

**So: a gap is a hole in the record. Not filmed is a book waiting for a camera. No link is a book waiting for a reader to travel, or an errand this archive has not run.**`))}></div>
    </div>

    <div class="one">
      <div class="lab">What a marker tells you now: the shelf, by record type, with the holes named</div>
      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={link(mdp(
`The panel used to list a parish's volumes in whatever order the providers were walked — births, births, deaths, births again — and a reader had to hold thirteen year-ranges in their head to see that nothing survives between two of them. **It now collapses them into a coverage timeline, one block per record type, and names the gaps.**

**${st.timelineGaps.places} of the ${s.placed} places on the map have a real hole in at least one register series** — ${st.timelineGaps.rows} of them in all. **${st.timelineGaps.clean} have none**: every year between their first volume and their last is covered by something.

*A one- or two-year hole is usually not a hole.* A volume titled «1716–1816» sitting beside one that ends in 1714 leaves 1715 uncovered on paper and almost certainly not in fact, so those are marked as a labelling artefact rather than cried over. **The exception is a parish whose volumes each cover a single year** — the Napoleonic comuni are all like this — where a missing year is a missing book. That rule is why [Mione's lost 1807](/carnia/#gap1807) shows on the map as three gaps and not as three shrugs.

**An index is not cover, and neither is an allegato.** They are counted separately at the foot of each panel, because a marriage index for 1815–1830 does not make the marriage book for those years present.`))}></div>
    </div>

    <div class="one">
      <div class="lab">The confessions, as David separated them</div>
      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={mdp(
`The map keeps his own layers, because the confession decides which archive holds the book and in what language it is written.

${layers.map(([k, n]) => `**${LAB[k] || k}** — ${n}`).join(" · ")}

Croatian registers are not all Catholic and a family that looks absent from one set can be sitting in another. **The ${s.byLayer.orth || 0} Orthodox parishes in particular are a body this archive has never touched.**`)}></div>
    </div>

    <div class="one">
      <div class="lab">And a layer that is not records at all</div>
      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={mdp(
`His map carries a tenth layer of **${s.researchers} Croatian genealogists** — each with a researcher number, the parish they work, their confession, and the surnames they are chasing. It is a directory of living people working the same ground, and it is **not on this map**, because people are not record books and because the archive's rule on living people applies to strangers as much as to family.

It is kept in the data, unpublished, and the useful question it answers is narrow: *is anybody else working our parishes?*`)}></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 style="font-size:30px;margin-top:50px">Our people: reading the second question</h2>

  <div class="grid g3" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="panel">
      <div class="lab">The line is short</div>
      <p class="dek" style="margin-top:8px">Nine places carry the whole documented descent, and seven of them sit inside a hundred kilometres of each other. The family did not move much until 1939, and then it moved to three continents in a decade.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <div class="lab">The cluster is not the line</div>
      <p class="dek" style="margin-top:8px">Rijeka, Pula and Ližnjan carry far more of the surname than Gračišće ever did — and none of them is connected to this family by any document. <strong>Weight on the map is not kinship.</strong></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <div class="lab">The gap is the argument</div>
      <p class="dek" style="margin-top:8px">Between Mione in 1679 and Gologorica in 1711 there is nothing but a house with a date on it. That two-hundred-kilometre jump is the one thing this archive cannot yet prove.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="one" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div class="lab">What a people marker means, and what it does not</div>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={link(mdp(
`A place is coloured under **our people** because somebody in the roster is attached to it — by a record, a headstone, a register or a family tree. **It does not mean that person was born there**, and where the archive has only a parish it has used the parish. Names in three languages are folded together: *Almissa* is Omiš, *Vitipolis* is Rijeka, *Promontore* is Premantura, *Spalato* is Split.

**${m.peoplePlaced} placements across ${m.people} towns**, and **${a.stats.withPeople}** of those towns carry at least one named person.

**Living people are on this map by name and by town, and by nothing else.** The rule until 15 September 2026 was *no place of residence*, which kept every living relative off the map entirely. It is now **name and town, never a date** — and **all ${liv} living people this archive names are placed**.

**${jhb} of them are at Johannesburg and ${bne} at Brisbane**, and two are at both. That is the shape of it: the family left Senj for the Rand, and **one household of the three went on to Australia** — the aunt and her husband, the uncle's widow and his daughters, and the cousins are all still in South Africa. **Not one of them has a date on this map**, and none ever will.`))}></div>
  </div>

  <h2 style="font-size:30px;margin-top:50px">The ground: the third question, and the finding in it</h2>

  <div class="one" style="border-left-color:var(--accent);margin-top:22px">
    <div class="lab">The two colours never meet, and that is the finding</div>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:8px" set:html={link(mdp(
`**${g.stats.stones} places have a photographed headstone. ${g.stats.register} have a burial register entry. Not one place has both.**

That is not a quirk of the data. It is the emigration, drawn: **the people who stayed are in parish books and have no findable grave; the people who left are under stones and were never in a Croatian register.** The register entries run **${g.stats.earliest} to 1997** and are almost all Istrian and Kvarner — Rijeka, Ližnjan, Poreč, Pula, Kaštelir. The headstones run **1844 to ${g.stats.latest}** and are almost all somewhere else — Connecticut, Rome, Queens, Broken Hill, Ticino.

**The green colour in that legend has no members.** It is left there because the day a place turns green — a grave found in a parish this archive has read — is the day one of these two halves joins the other.

**${g.stats.unplacedRegister} burials have no place to pin.** Thirty-six are entered as *«Croatia, parish not named»* and four as *«not stated»*, read out of indexes that record a death without recording where. They are counted on [the burials page](/burials/) and deliberately absent here: **a map that guessed at a parish would be worse than a map with a hole in it.**`))}></div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="biggest" style="font-size:26px;margin-top:44px">The twelve heaviest places in the ground</h2>
  <p class="dek" style="margin-top:8px">Where most of the family lies, by how many burials this archive can name there.</p>
